WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.787
A warehouse sends parcels by van to a shop far away.

2
00:00:05.787 --> 00:00:08.281
It doesn't wait for each van to come back.

3
00:00:08.281 --> 00:00:14.219
It keeps several on the road at once, and each signed note from the shop frees a place for one more.

4
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:20.839
In TCP, the vans are segments, its packets, and how many may be out at once is the window.

5
00:00:20.839 --> 00:00:22.413
More vans, more speed.

6
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.622
The shop says how much room it has.

7
00:00:24.622 --> 00:00:27.230
But nobody says how much the road can take.

8
00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.837
So how many vans should the warehouse send?

9
00:00:29.967 --> 00:00:33.458
Somewhere on the way is the narrowest part, the bottleneck.

10
00:00:33.458 --> 00:00:37.127
Picture a narrow bridge, with a small car park in front of it.

11
00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:41.774
Vans that arrive faster than the bridge lets them over wait in the car park.

12
00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:46.816
When the car park is full, the next vans are turned away, and their parcels are lost.

13
00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:50.414
In a network, the bridge is the slowest link on the path.

14
00:00:50.414 --> 00:00:55.434
The car park is the queue in the router in front of it, a waiting area for packets.

15
00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:57.248
And turned away means dropped.

16
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.978
Here, the road and the car park together hold 100 vans.

17
00:01:00.978 --> 00:01:03.515
The warehouse doesn't know that number.

18
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.096
So parcels get lost.

19
00:01:05.096 --> 00:01:08.377
The obvious fix: send them again, just as fast.

20
00:01:08.377 --> 00:01:10.680
But the car park is already full.

21
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.799
Resending at the same speed only adds more vans to the jam.

22
00:01:14.933 --> 00:01:19.019
In October 1986, the early internet did exactly that.

23
00:01:19.019 --> 00:01:26.651
A link between two university sites at Berkeley fell from 32 kilobits a second to 40 bits a second.

24
00:01:26.651 --> 00:01:28.116
Bits, not kilobits.

25
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.815
That's called congestion collapse.

26
00:01:30.815 --> 00:01:34.957
The fix came from Van Jacobson, no relation to our vans.

27
00:01:34.957 --> 00:01:40.800
It's called congestion control, and it's been part of TCP since the late 1980s.

28
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:50.744
The idea: the sender keeps its own guess of how much the road can take, and never has more on the road than that guess, or the shop's room, whichever is smaller.

29
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:52.926
Nobody tells it the right number.

30
00:01:52.926 --> 00:01:57.591
So it tries, and it treats a lost parcel as the signal that it went too far.

31
00:01:57.767 --> 00:02:02.943
A new connection starts small, at 10 segments, about 14 kilobytes on Linux.

32
00:02:02.943 --> 00:02:06.186
Each signed note that comes back adds one more.

33
00:02:06.186 --> 00:02:09.499
So 10 out means 10 notes back, and 20 next time.

34
00:02:09.633 --> 00:02:10.789
Then 40, then 80.

35
00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:13.167
The guess doubles every round trip.

36
00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:14.934
This is called slow start.

37
00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:17.788
Nobody promised the names would be honest.

38
00:02:18.167 --> 00:02:19.920
80 still fits in 100.

39
00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.342
Next round, it doubles again.

40
00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:23.428
What happens?

41
00:02:23.428 --> 00:02:24.764
Pause and guess.

42
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:28.775
160 vans.

43
00:02:28.775 --> 00:02:32.221
100 fit on the road and in the car park.

44
00:02:32.221 --> 00:02:34.719
The other 60 are turned away.

45
00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.738
How does the warehouse find out?

46
00:02:36.738 --> 00:02:39.379
The parcels behind the lost ones still arrive.

47
00:02:39.379 --> 00:02:43.859
So the shop keeps sending the same note: I'm still missing the one after this.

48
00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:49.752
Three repeats of the same note, three duplicate ACKs, say exactly one parcel went missing.

49
00:02:49.752 --> 00:02:53.756
The sender resends it at once, which is called fast retransmit.

50
00:02:53.756 --> 00:02:56.107
And it cuts its guess in half, to 80.

51
00:02:56.233 --> 00:03:00.607
Above that point, it grows gently: one more segment per round trip.

52
00:03:00.607 --> 00:03:03.415
Twenty round trips later, it's back at 100.

53
00:03:03.567 --> 00:03:08.862
At 101, the car park overflows again, and the guess drops by half, to 50.

54
00:03:08.862 --> 00:03:12.199
Then up by one, down by half, again and again.

55
00:03:12.367 --> 00:03:16.757
The pattern has a name: additive increase, multiplicative decrease.

56
00:03:16.757 --> 00:03:19.313
Add one at a time, cut in half at once.

57
00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:22.276
The sender's guess is the congestion window.

58
00:03:22.276 --> 00:03:24.446
On the chart, it draws a sawtooth.

59
00:03:24.446 --> 00:03:28.724
On average, it sits at about three quarters of what the road holds.

60
00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.339
And if nothing comes back at all, not even a repeat, that's worse news.

61
00:03:33.339 --> 00:03:36.465
The guess drops to 1, and slow start begins again.

62
00:03:36.633 --> 00:03:37.600
Two warnings.

63
00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.839
First, Linux doesn't run this plain version, called Reno.

64
00:03:41.839 --> 00:03:49.128
Its default since 2006 is CUBIC, which cuts to 70 percent, not half, and grows back along a curve.

65
00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:51.359
Same idea, a flatter sawtooth.

66
00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:53.301
Second, loss is expensive.

67
00:03:53.301 --> 00:03:59.742
On the lab network, with a 50 millisecond delay, a 10 megabyte download took about 2 seconds.

68
00:03:59.742 --> 00:04:03.552
With one packet in a hundred dropped, it took about 20.

69
00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:08.272
So when a fast line feels slow, look for loss before you buy a bigger road.

70
00:04:08.667 --> 00:04:09.857
So, how many vans?

71
00:04:09.857 --> 00:04:11.841
The sender can't see the road.

72
00:04:11.841 --> 00:04:17.199
It feels for the limit: grow until a parcel is lost, cut in half, and grow again.

73
00:04:17.367 --> 00:04:21.427
Everyone sharing the road does the same, and that's what keeps it moving.

74
00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:22.783
One quick check.

75
00:04:22.783 --> 00:04:26.407
The guess is 90 when three duplicate ACKs arrive.

76
00:04:26.407 --> 00:04:27.590
What is it next?

77
00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:28.773
Pause and think.

78
00:04:31.967 --> 00:04:32.252
45.

79
00:04:32.252 --> 00:04:33.299
Half of 90.

80
00:04:33.299 --> 00:04:36.915
Then it climbs by one each round trip.
